 @Hisokaa-d2j  I don't know what your point is. The value comparison is far worse than it has been in the past & that's a fact.

"plenty disagree" sure. Availability is down, manufacturing limited, crypto booming, and the market itself significantly larger due to covid lockdown. There's no reason for Nvidia or AMD to sell good value products right now when anything they put out will sell. Even the 3090 is selling.

As for the 3080 outperforming a console -yeah  you'd hope so, in real terms it's over double the price of a whole console for a single component. But what does the 3060 bring that the past 4 years of GPUs haven't?

 @S3lvah  yeah my 970 has been hurting quite a bit, a 1070 or 5700 would have been much better to be left with.

Intel's been flagging behind in the GPU space but there's a big opportunity for them to come in and disrupt the GPU market in a big way if the current duopoly don't.

TSMC's defacto silicon manufacturing monopoly on this end of the market is a clear bottleneck on production for the whole industry though, even if crypto crashes it's hard to see things changing much without their capacity, or 3rd party competition stepping up. Their US facility is still a couple of years away at the earliest, so it might be time for me to either pick a new hobby, or stick to indies & my backlog at least until PC gpu prices are no longer ridiculous.

 @FzNd  yeah I felt the squeeze when Half-Life Alyx came out & my paltry 4GB of VRAM didn't meet minimum spec. Though LowSpecGamer got it working on less, that is one game where I really do want to experience the game in its best state, so I'm waiting to upgrade for that.

Since the Vram is the main issue with the card's longevity & a 980 would see me in a much better position right now, I'm also currently aiming at a 3080 or 6800XT as once time goes on, I can always drop res & still get great visuals, but like you say, not at that price. within $50 of MSRP maybe, but no more. I'll wait for RDNA3 before I pay the current going rate. My steam backlog is huge & indies are great.

New levels of graphics & performance are good & all, but there's only so much I'm willing to spend on this hobby. And the current difference between MSRP & real-world sale price for these cards could buy me a 5600X which would easily see me through the next 5 years on the CPU side.

 @surge9310  Yeah, it's still in 3rd place behind 1366x768 (a laptop screen resolution) and 1080p. & like you say, a 5-year-old GPU is perfectly sufficient at those resolutions for most games even today if you're willing to bump some settings down.

Honestly, for most desktop monitor sizes & purposes, I see 4K as a useless extravagance way past the point of diminishing returns, and the only reason I'd buy into it is that OLED TVs still aren't being made for resolutions below 4K.

My main reason for even considering a GPU that can run games well at 4K today - is so it can run games well at 1440p 3-4 years from now. But with prices the way they are, and games that leverage the new console hardware still being extremely thin on the ground (majority of titles still being crossgen), I'm thinking I may as well just wait another year or so.

 @stevebalmer8888  He was probably blinded by how "great" Jensen painted Ampere, by comparing 2080 (TU-104 die) vs 3080 (GA-102 die). In reality Ampere is just a meh generational improvement for gaming. Thou program that use CUDA does benefit alot from the doubling of CUDA core.
.
If we compare the actual GPU die, it should have been TU-102 vs GA-102 (aka 2080ti vs 3080). The different is meh, just like how this 3060 is vs the 2060 (GA-106 vs TU-106). Factoring in Ampere card using more power than Turing, it's even more meh tbh. The only saving grace of Ampere is it's more "reasonable" priced. But that's also because Turing price was Ridiculus to begin with.
